# Commit and push when a task finishes

When a coding task is complete (implementation done, related docs/kanban updated, checks you ran look good):

1. **Commit** all relevant changes on `dev` using the repo commit protocol (status, diff, log in parallel; HEREDOC message focused on why; never skip hooks; never commit secrets).
2. **Push** immediately to `origin/dev` (`git push -u origin HEAD` if upstream is missing).
3. Do this **without waiting** for the user to ask — finishing the task includes commit + push.
4. If the working tree is clean, skip an empty commit and still confirm the branch is pushed/up to date.
5. If push fails (auth/network), report the error and the unpushed commit SHA; do not silently drop the commit.

This project rule overrides generic “only commit when asked” guidance for Closeloop work.
